From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anthoine Bourgeois <anthoine.bourgeois@vates.tech>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 10:11:03 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] vhd-util: coalesce: merge several blocks into the parent at
 once

The parent was written by the main thread alone, one synchronous I/O at
a time: the bitmap read and write of a block merged into an allocated
parent block, or the runs of a partial new block, each waited for a full
round trip while the disk had nothing else to do.

Merge the child blocks with 4 writer threads instead, taking the slots
filled by the reader in order, with 8 slots so that the reader still
reads ahead. Each parent block is written by a single writer. The end
of data is reserved under the lock, the batmap is updated under it, and
each writer gets its own parent bitmap buffer.

The BAT entries of new blocks are queued with their batmap bit and only
applied to the in-memory BAT when written out, under a flush lock that
serializes the flushes: the fdatasync() then covers the data of every
entry written, and BAT sectors reach the disk in the order of their
contents. The first error stops the reader and the other writers; the
BAT entries of the blocks merged so far are still written.

Several writers are only used for raw, fixed and block device parents.
With a file, the footer written after each new block may land over the
beginning of the next one being written, and the generic path through
vhd_io_write(), for a different block size, is not thread-safe.

Tested with 4 writers forced on file parents, under ThreadSanitizer,
with full, partial and sparse child blocks, and with pwrite() failures
injected: the parent passes vhd-util check and matches the expected
contents, including after a coalesce retried after a failure.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
